# Shorter sentences for the 10-year-old reading level. Sources are unchanged.
NEW = {
"What is a SSAS?": ["A SSAS is a pension scheme set up by an employer. The employer is usually a company owned and run by its directors. The law calls this an occupational pension scheme. The members can decide how the money is invested, within HMRC's rules. Once HMRC registers it, it gets the pension tax reliefs."],
"What does SSAS stand for?": ["SSAS stands for small self-administered scheme. The name comes from tax rules made in 1991. They covered schemes with fewer than 12 members that invested at least some of their money outside insurance policies. Those rules ended on 6 April 2006, when today's pension tax rules began. Today's tax law does not define a SSAS. The FCA's rulebook still describes one."],
"Is a SSAS an occupational pension scheme?": ["Yes. Both the tax law and the pensions law treat it as one. That is because an employer sets it up to provide pensions for people who work for it. It is also why The Pensions Regulator, not the FCA, oversees SSASs."],
"Who can set one up?": ["An employer sets up a SSAS. For most people reading this, that is their own company. The scheme administrator then applies online to HMRC to register it. HMRC can ask questions, and it can refuse. For example, it can refuse if the company has been dormant for a month in a row in the past year."],
"Does a SSAS need a sponsoring employer?": ["Yes. A SSAS is set up by an employer. Any employer that sets up or joins the scheme is called a sponsoring employer. If a sponsoring employer is a dormant company, HMRC can refuse to register the SSAS. It can also take an existing registration away."],
"Can I set up a SSAS if I am the only director?": ["Yes. A SSAS can have just one member. A one-member SSAS does not have to register with The Pensions Regulator. It still needs HMRC registration to get the tax reliefs. Some pension law rules do not apply to a one-member scheme, such as having a scheme actuary or meeting the scheme funding rules."],
"How many members can a SSAS have?": ["The law sets no top limit, but 12 is the number that matters. Some pension law rules do not apply to a scheme with fewer than 12 members, if every member is a trustee. The trustees must also decide everything together, or have an independent trustee. Such a scheme needs no auditor, and the scheme funding rules do not apply. Nor do the pension law limits on investing in the employer. HMRC's tax rules apply whatever the size."],
"Who can be a member?": ["It depends on the scheme's own rules. A SSAS is set up to give pensions to people who work for the company. But the law also lets it pay benefits to other people. Members can live anywhere. Tax relief on a member's own payments depends on being a relevant UK individual, such as someone with UK earnings. A member who is not a trustee can mean the scheme loses some small-scheme exemptions."],
"Can my family be members?": ["Yes, if the scheme's rules allow it. But check the tax first. Your company might pay into the SSAS for a family member who does not work for it. If so, HMRC taxes that payment as a benefit of the employee it is linked to. The company also pays National Insurance on it. If a family member does work for the company, their pay and pension together should be fair for the work they do."],
"Can several directors put their pensions into one SSAS?": ["Yes. One SSAS can have several members, such as all the directors. Each member has their own share in the scheme, which HMRC calls an arrangement. In a money purchase SSAS, each member's pension comes from their own pot. A SSAS with more than one member must register with The Pensions Regulator within three months."],
"Is the SSAS dying out?": ["The official figures do not show that. In June 2026 the government said there are around 60,000 SSAS savers across 21,000 schemes. The Pensions Regulator counted 21,260 relevant small schemes in 2025. That is its name for schemes of this kind. It said the number has stayed broadly the same since 2015."],
"What is the difference between a trustee, a scheme administrator and a practitioner?": ["The trustees hold the scheme's money and assets on trust for the members. The scheme administrator is the person or firm responsible for the pension tax rules and for dealing with HMRC. HMRC uses the word practitioner for someone who acts for the scheme administrator on HMRC's online service, such as an accountant. In the SSAS world, practitioner often just means the specialist firm that helps run the scheme. That is not a legal term."],
"Do I need a professional trustee?": ["No law says a SSAS must have one today. Before 6 April 2006, the Inland Revenue would only approve a SSAS if one trustee was a pensioneer trustee it had approved. That rule ended when today's pension tax rules began. An independent trustee is now one way to qualify for some small-scheme exemptions. The other way is for the member trustees to decide everything together. Some SSAS firms will only run a scheme if they are a trustee too."],
"Can I run a SSAS without a professional SSAS provider?": ["Yes, the law allows it. The scheme administrator can be one or more individuals. So the member trustees can take on the role themselves. Since 6 April 2026 a scheme administrator must be UK resident. They must also make declarations to HMRC and be fit and proper. HMRC may decide someone is not fit and proper if they lack enough knowledge of pensions tax law and do not use an adviser who has it."],
"Are SSAS trustees personally liable if something goes wrong?": ["Yes, they can be. A trustee can have to pay for losses caused by a breach of trust out of their own money. The trustees share that risk. The trust deed cannot remove liability for failing to take care over investments. Fines from The Pensions Regulator must be paid personally, not from the scheme. Trustees who are also the scheme administrator are liable for some tax charges, such as the scheme sanction charge."],
"Who regulates a SSAS?": ["The Pensions Regulator regulates workplace pension schemes, including SSASs. HMRC registers them and runs the pension tax rules. The FCA does not regulate running a SSAS. But it does regulate firms that give advice on investments and on pension transfers. Complaints about how a SSAS is run can go to the Pensions Ombudsman. Complaints about financial advice go to the Financial Ombudsman Service."],
"Is a SSAS protected by the FSCS?": ["The scheme itself is not. The FSCS says it cannot protect occupational pension schemes if they fail. But money a SSAS keeps in a UK bank can be protected, if the employer is a micro, small or medium sized business. For a money purchase SSAS, the FSCS protects each eligible member up to £120,000 per bank or banking group. If a regulated adviser gave bad pension advice and has since failed, the FSCS may pay up to £85,000."],
"What happens to my SSAS if the provider or administrator goes bust?": ["A SSAS's money and assets are held on trust by its trustees. They do not belong to the firm that does the paperwork. Hartley Pensions went into administration on 29 July 2022. The FCA said the pension assets were held by trustee firms that had not gone bust. But moving to a new administrator can be slow and costly. In July 2026, four years on, Hartley's administrators said its SSAS business had still not been sold. They also said a court order would be needed before clients could be charged a fee to move out."],
"Does a final salary SSAS need an actuary?": ["Usually, yes. Pension law says every occupational pension scheme must appoint an actuary, unless regulations exempt it. Money purchase schemes are exempt. So are schemes with fewer than 2 members. So a final salary (defined benefit) SSAS with 2 or more members must appoint an actuary. That is so even though it can be let off the auditor and the scheme funding rules. A one-member final salary SSAS is exempt from all three."],
"What is the difference between a SSAS and a SIPP?": ["A SIPP is a personal pension, and the firm that runs it needs FCA permission. A SSAS is an occupational pension scheme that a company sets up. Its members are usually its trustees, so they run it together. Both can buy commercial property. But only an occupational scheme like a SSAS can lend money to the company behind it, under HMRC's strict rules."],
"Can I move a SIPP into a SSAS?": ["The law allows money to move from one registered pension scheme to another. This is called a recognised transfer, and it can be cash or other assets. Before the money goes, the SIPP firm must run scam checks. A SSAS is an occupational scheme, so you must show a real link to its employer. Three months of payslips is one example. Serious warning signs (red flags) mean the transfer must stop. Lesser ones (amber flags) mean you must first have a guidance session with MoneyHelper."],
"Can I move a SSAS into a SIPP?": ["Yes. A SIPP is also a registered pension scheme, so this can be a recognised transfer. Assets the SSAS owns, such as business property, can move across as they are (in specie), if the SIPP firm accepts them. Or they can be sold first and the cash moved. A SIPP cannot lend to your company without a tax charge. So ask the SSAS administrator first how any loan to your company must be dealt with."],
"How much can be paid in each year?": ["For 2026/27 the annual allowance is £60,000. That is the most that can go into your pensions in a year before a tax charge may apply. It counts what you, your company and anyone else pay in. You only get tax relief on your own payments up to your UK earnings for the year, or £3,600 if that is more. Your company's payments are not limited to your salary. But they must be paid wholly and exclusively for the business.", "The allowance is lower if your threshold income is over £200,000 and your adjusted income is over £260,000. It never goes below £10,000. It is also £10,000 for money purchase savings once you have taken money flexibly from a pension."],
"What is carry forward?": ["Carry forward lets you add annual allowance you did not use in the last 3 tax years to this year's allowance. You must have been a member of a registered pension scheme at some time in each of those years. It does not matter if nothing was paid in. This year's allowance is used first, then the spare allowance from the oldest year. Carry forward cannot be added to the £10,000 money purchase annual allowance."],
"Does my company get corporation tax relief?": ["Usually, yes. Your company can deduct its pension payments from its profits. It gets the relief for the accounting period in which it actually pays them. HMRC accepts this unless there is a non-business reason for the payment. One example is a director's total pay being too high for the work they do.", "Very large increases can be spread. If one year's payments are more than 2.1 times the year before, relief on an increase of £500,000 or more is spread over 2, 3 or 4 years. Paying in an asset instead of money only counts as a money contribution if it is set up in a particular way. There must be papers to prove each step."],
"Can I move my old pensions into a SSAS?": ["The law allows money to move from one registered pension scheme to another. A SSAS can accept it if its rules allow. Before your old scheme lets the money go, it must run the scam checks in the 2021 transfer rules. These include proof that you have a real link to the SSAS's employer. Anyone who advises you on a transfer, or on where to invest your pension, must have the right FCA permission. Moving can mean losing valuable benefits or guarantees in your old scheme."],
"Can I move a final salary pension into a SSAS?": ["A final salary pension promises a set income. The law calls this a safeguarded benefit. If it is worth more than £30,000, the old scheme's trustees must check you have had advice from an authorised independent adviser. They must check this before they move it. The adviser's firm must have the FCA permission for advising on pension transfers. FCA rules tell advisers to start by assuming a transfer will not be suitable. This site does not give advice on pension transfers."],
"Why does the government want a new red flag for transfers into a SSAS?": ["Because it is worried that fraudsters misuse SSASs. On 9 June 2026 the Department for Work and Pensions (DWP) asked for views on a new red flag. If you could not show a real job link with the SSAS's employer, the transfer would be stopped. The DWP says there are on average over 100 transfers into SSASs a year. It says up to 1 in 10 could be fraud.", "It also says pension fraud victims lost £18,400 on average in 2024/25. Where an investment was the main vehicle for the fraud, the average was £38,400. The consultation closed on 21 July 2026. By 5 October 2026 the government had not published its response. So this is a proposal, not yet law."],
"What can a SSAS invest in?": ["HMRC's tax rules do not limit the kinds of asset a SSAS can buy. But some bring heavy tax charges. These are homes, and physical things you can move, such as art, antiques or classic cars. Pension law also gives the trustees duties. Before investing, they must get and consider proper advice, given or confirmed in writing. They must also think about spreading the risk."],
"Can my SSAS buy my business premises?": ["Yes. A SSAS can buy commercial property, such as an office, shop, factory or warehouse. That includes the building your company uses. Commercial property is not taxable property, so the heavy charges for homes do not apply. The SSAS might buy from you, your company or anyone connected with you. If so, it must pay the price an unconnected buyer would pay."],
"Can my SSAS buy the premises from me?": ["Yes, but only at full market value. If the SSAS pays you more than the property is worth, HMRC treats the extra as an unauthorised payment and taxes it. The same applies if the SSAS later sells to you or your company for less than it is worth. Stamp duty land tax, or the Scottish or Welsh version, is due in the normal way."],
"Can my company pay rent to my SSAS?": ["Yes. Your company can rent a building the SSAS owns, but it must pay the full commercial rent. If it pays less, HMRC taxes the shortfall as an unauthorised payment. Rent that is left unpaid can be treated as a loan to the company, if it is not collected on normal terms. A loan to the company must meet strict rules. So the SSAS must collect the rent like any other landlord, with no special treatment for the company."],
"Does a SSAS-owned property need an EPC rating?": ["Usually, yes. A commercial building needs an Energy Performance Certificate (EPC) when it is sold or let. An EPC lasts 10 years. In England and Wales, a landlord must not let a commercial building rated below E, unless a valid exemption is registered. Since 1 April 2023 this covers existing lettings too. The SSAS trustees own the building, so they are the landlord.", "The government plans that from 2031, rented buildings over 1,000 square metres must reach B where cost effective. That still needs new law. Its earlier plan for a C rating by 2027 has been dropped. Scotland and Northern Ireland have their own rules."],
"Can a SSAS buy land and develop commercial property?": ["In principle, yes. The tax rules do not stop a SSAS buying land, or putting up a commercial building to rent out. But if it builds in order to sell at a profit, HMRC may see that as trading. Trading profits do not get the pension scheme tax exemptions. Homes are a bigger risk. Once a new or converted building is ready to live in while the SSAS owns it, it counts as residential property. Then the heavy charges apply."],
"Can a SSAS own residential property?": ["It can, but homes are taxable property and the tax charges are heavy. The member can face a 40% charge, with a possible 15% surcharge. The scheme faces a scheme sanction charge. There are also yearly charges while the scheme holds the home, and a charge on any gain.", "Some buildings do not count as homes. Examples are a care home, a student hall, a hospital or a whole hotel. A flat for an employee who must live there for the job does not count either. Nor does a flat that goes with business premises the SSAS owns. Both only work if no member or connected person lives there."],
"Can my SSAS borrow?": ["Yes. A SSAS can borrow up to 50% of its net value just before the new borrowing. Any borrowing it already has counts towards that limit. HMRC lets it borrow for any purpose the trustees are satisfied will benefit the scheme. If it borrows more than the limit, the scheme administrator pays a 40% scheme sanction charge on the excess. Pension law's own limit on borrowing does not apply to schemes with fewer than 100 members."],
"Can my SSAS lend money to my company?": ["Yes, if your company is a sponsoring employer and the loan meets HMRC's strict rules (see <a href=\"#what-are-the-loanback-rules\">the loanback rules</a>). The SSAS cannot lend to you, to any other member, or to anyone connected with a member. Pension law also bans loans to the employer unless the SSAS is a small scheme. That means fewer than 12 members, all of them trustees. The trustees must also decide everything together, or have an independent trustee."],
"What are the loanback rules?": ["A loan to a sponsoring employer can be no more than 50% of the SSAS's net value when it is made. It must be secured by a first charge for its whole term. The asset charged must be worth at least the loan plus interest. Interest must be at least 1% above the average base rate of six named banks. That average is first rounded up to the next 0.25%. The loan must be repaid within five years, in at least equal yearly amounts of capital and interest. HMRC's guidance does not say what the company may spend the money on. But every loan must be a genuine investment: prudent, secure and commercial."],
"What happens if a loanback is not repaid?": ["Sometimes a company is in genuine difficulty and money is still owed when the loan ends. Then the loan can be rolled over once, for up to five more years, and the existing security can carry on. A loan rolled over more than once falls under the unauthorised payment rules. In HMRC's own example the company pays a 40% charge. The scheme administrator can also face a scheme sanction charge of 15% to 40%. The trustees must chase the debt as any other creditor would, using the security if needed."],
"Can my SSAS lend to somebody else's company?": ["Yes. HMRC has no objection to a SSAS lending to people or businesses that are not connected with the members or the sponsoring employer. The loan should be a genuine investment: prudent, secure and on normal commercial terms. Connected is a wide idea. It includes close relatives, husbands, wives and civil partners and their relatives, and companies any of them control. A loan to a connected person is an unauthorised payment and is taxed."],
"Can my SSAS buy shares in my company?": ["Yes, within a limit. When the SSAS buys them, the shares must be worth less than 5% of the SSAS's net assets. With several sponsoring employers, the total must be under 20%, and under 5% in any one. If a limit is broken, the company pays a 40% tax charge. The scheme faces a scheme sanction charge. Pension law has its own 5% limit, but it does not apply to a small scheme. And if your company owns a home, the SSAS can be treated as owning part of it. That brings the taxable property charges."],
"Can a SSAS buy shares in an unquoted company?": ["Yes. HMRC says pension schemes can buy shares whether or not they are quoted on a stock exchange. The seller might be a member, the employer or someone connected with them. If so, the price must be what an unconnected buyer would pay. If the company owns homes, the SSAS can be treated as owning part of them. That does not apply to a trading company if three things are true. The SSAS and people linked to it do not control it. No member or connected person is a controlling director. And the shares are not held so that such a person can use the property."],
"Can a SSAS invest overseas?": ["Yes. The tax rules do not limit the kinds of asset a SSAS can hold. HMRC says schemes can buy shares in UK or overseas companies. The taxable property rules apply anywhere in the world. So a house, flat or holiday home abroad brings the same heavy charges as one in the UK."],
"Can a SSAS pay for life assurance?": ["Yes. HMRC says pension schemes can use insurance policies, such as term life cover, to fund the death benefits they promise. Any payout goes through the scheme. It is paid under the scheme's rules as a lump sum death benefit. A member's own contributions used to pay premiums on a personal (non-group) life policy get no tax relief. From 6 April 2027, death in service benefits paid by a registered pension scheme stay outside inheritance tax, if they meet the conditions."],
"When can I take money out?": ["Normally from age 55. From 6 April 2028 this goes up to 57. You may be able to start sooner if you are too ill to carry on your job. You may also have a protected pension age. If its rules allow, a SSAS can pay out in several ways. These include a tax-free lump sum, money taken out bit by bit (drawdown), a scheme pension, or buying an annuity."],
"How much can I take tax free?": ["Usually up to 25% of the amount built up in your pension. There is also a top limit, called the lump sum allowance. Since 6 April 2024 it is £268,275 for most people, in total across all their pensions. Some people with older protections can take more. The rest is taxed as income when it is paid out."],
"Is there an age limit for a SSAS?": ["No. You can be a member of a SSAS at any age. But you only get tax relief on money you pay in yourself if you are under 75. You must also be a relevant UK individual, such as someone with UK earnings. Since April 2011 you do not have to buy an annuity at 75."],
"What happens to my SSAS if I die before 75?": ["The people who get your SSAS money usually pay no income tax on it. A lump sum is tax free if it is paid within two years of the scheme learning of your death. It must also fit within your lump sum and death benefit allowance, usually £1,073,100. Money they draw out bit by bit (drawdown) is usually tax free too. From 6 April 2027, inheritance tax may also apply."],
"What happens if I die at 75 or over?": ["The people who get your SSAS money pay income tax on it, at their own tax rate. If a lump sum is paid to a company, or to most trusts, the scheme pays a 45% tax charge instead. From 6 April 2027, inheritance tax may apply as well. But income tax is not charged on the part used to pay the inheritance tax."],
"Will my SSAS face inheritance tax from April 2027?": ["It may. If you die on or after 6 April 2027, most money left in your SSAS counts as part of your estate for inheritance tax. Tax at 40% is only due on the part of your estate above your tax-free amount. Money left to a husband, wife or civil partner is usually tax free. A few benefits are left out, such as a dependants' scheme pension and some death in service benefits.", "Your personal representatives must report and pay the tax. They can ask the scheme to pay it straight to HMRC. Steve explains it in full in his guide to <a href=\"https://inheritancetaxexplained.co.uk/pensions-and-inheritance-tax-from-april-2027/guide/\">pensions and inheritance tax from April 2027</a>."],
"What is a dependants' scheme pension, and why does it matter?": ["It is a regular income the scheme pays to a dependant after a member dies. A dependant includes a husband, wife or civil partner, or a child under 23. It also includes someone who relied on the member financially. It matters because of inheritance tax. From 6 April 2027, money that may only be used to pay a dependants' scheme pension is left out of the inheritance tax sums. The dependant still pays income tax on the pension, whatever age the member was at death."],
"How much does a SSAS cost to set up and run?": ["We checked the current fees of ten providers. For a SSAS with two members, the yearly fee is £800 to £2,420 plus VAT. Most also charge initial costs. Of those we can price, the highest in our simple example is £2,100 plus VAT. The fees are flat fees, not a percentage of the pension. See <a href=\"/ssas-pension-table-showing-best-ssas-providers/\">the full fee comparison</a> for what each provider charges."],
"Is there a minimum amount for a SSAS to be worth it?": ["No law sets a minimum size for a SSAS. But the fees are flat, so the smaller the pension, the bigger the fee is as a share of it. Compare a 1% yearly charge. A flat fee of £1,500 a year costs less once the pension is above £150,000, and more below it. Some providers set their own minimum. Howden only sets up schemes of at least £200,000, and Alltrust's minimum is £100,000."],
"How long does it take to set up?": ["There is no fixed time. First the scheme administrator must be registered with HMRC. Then they apply online to register the scheme. HMRC may ask questions, and it has no time limit to decide. If there is no decision within six months, the administrator can appeal to a tribunal. Tax relief only starts from the date HMRC registers the scheme. A SSAS with more than one member must also register with The Pensions Regulator within three months."],
"Does HMRC still register new SSASs, and why does it refuse some?": ["Yes. The law says HMRC must register a new pension scheme unless certain problems apply. One is wrong or false information. Another is a scheme not set up mainly to pay pensions. Others are a scheme administrator who is not fit and proper, or a sponsoring company that has been dormant for a month in a row in the past year. HMRC does refuse many. Its April 2026 newsletter said it had refused 33% of 2,421 applications to register new pension schemes. That figure covers all kinds of scheme, not just SSASs."],
"What does the trustee have to do each year?": ["Keep clear records. Keep them for the tax year they relate to and six more years. File with HMRC when needed. That means an event report by 31 January if certain events happened, and a pension scheme return if HMRC sends a notice to file. It also means an Accounting for Tax return if certain tax charges arise. A SSAS with 2 to 11 members files a scheme return with The Pensions Regulator every three years. A small SSAS needs no auditor, if every member is a trustee and the trustees decide everything together or have an independent trustee."],
"What happens to the SSAS if my company is sold?": ["It depends on the SSAS's own rules. The scheme's assets are held by its trustees, not by the company. The rules may let a different company become the sponsoring employer. The trustees must tell The Pensions Regulator about changes to employer details as soon as reasonably practicable. Members can usually move their share to another registered pension scheme. Any loan from the SSAS to the company must keep to HMRC's loan rules until it is paid back."],
"What happens if my company closes?": ["A SSAS does not end just because the company closes, unless its rules say so. The trustees may be able to run it as a closed scheme, or they may wind it up. HMRC can take a SSAS off its register if a sponsoring employer is a dormant company. That brings a tax charge of 40% of everything the scheme holds. If the SSAS is wound up, HMRC must be told within three months of the wind up finishing."],
"What are the warning signs of a SSAS scam?": ["Be wary of anyone who contacts you out of the blue about your pension. Cold calls about pensions are against the law, apart from narrow exceptions. Other warning signs are promises of high returns, offers to get cash out before age 55, pressure to sign quickly, and unusual or overseas investments. For a SSAS, one big warning sign is being asked to join the scheme of a company you have no real link to. Another is being helped to invent a job link, which MoneyHelper says is fraud. Check any firm on the <a href=\"https://register.fca.org.uk/\">FCA Register</a>. Never take advice from a company that contacted you."],
"What are the disadvantages of a SSAS?": ["A SSAS costs money to set up and run each year. The fees are flat, so they weigh more on a small pension. The trustees, often the members themselves, must follow pension and tax law. They can be personally liable for losses they cause. Break the tax rules and the charges are heavy: 40% on the payment, and a scheme sanction charge of up to 40% on top. A loan to your own company can go wrong. And the FSCS cannot protect the scheme itself if it fails. See <a href=\"/ssas-disadvantages/\">the full list of disadvantages</a>."],
}
lines=open('faq.txt',encoding='utf-8').read().splitlines()
out=[];i=0;seen=set()
while i<len(lines):
    l=lines[i]
    if l.startswith('## '):
        q=l[3:].strip(); assert q in NEW, q; seen.add(q)
        out.append(l); i+=1
        while i<len(lines) and not lines[i].startswith('SRC:'):
            i+=1
        out.extend(NEW[q])
        continue
    out.append(l); i+=1
assert seen==set(NEW), set(NEW)-seen
open('faq.txt','w',encoding='utf-8').write('\n'.join(out)+'\n')
print('rewrote',len(seen),'answers')
